## Experimental definition and functional bearing

The reference is C.kluyveri DSM555 NfnAB coexpressed in E.coli, with an
N-terminal NfnA His6 tag and C-terminal NfnB His6 tag, purified anoxically and
FeS-reconstituted. NfnA/B coexist as the studied complex. They are not variants
replacing one another. Exact assayed preparation IDs, active fraction and
per-estimate purity/replication are not established. Separate expressed or
reconstituted subunits are separate experimental arrangements, not matched
replicate rates of the successful coexpressed complex.

The selected reports are apparent fitted specific activities, rounded after
Lineweaver–Burk extrapolation:23 U/mg for ferredoxin reduction at430nm and8 U/mg
for NADH formation at380nm. One U refers to1µmol/min of the particular endpoint
species. The denominator is protein mass, not assayed molecule amount; these
numbers must not enter kcat/per-molecule fields. Both use the reported basal
37°C/pH7 conditions and NADPH regeneration, but ferredoxin concentration is
50µM versus10µM, hydrogenase is added to the380nm assay, and redox dynamics differ.
The ferredoxin/hydrogenase partners are from C.pasteurianum. This is experimental
context, not a computed homology or target-transfer score.

Reaction5 links one two-electron ferredoxin and one NADH per two NADPH in its
backward direction. That supports a stoichiometric relationship; it does not
establish one common-condition unbiased rate. The source explicitly identifies
overlapping ferredoxin absorption as preventing accurate NADH-rate determination.
The reported8 therefore remains an apparent source estimate, not an isolated
physical NADH flux. Existence/dependence and coupled-reaction assertions can
retain support while quantitative turnover is qualified.

